    "answer": {
      "short": "Let $D=ad-bc$.\n\nFor $c\\ne0$ and $D\\ne0$: a single interval when $k\\leq \\frac{a}{c}$; two disjoint intervals when $k>\\frac{a}{c}$.\n\nFor $c\\ne0$ and $D=0$: two intervals, $\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{-\\frac{d}{c}\\}$, when $k>\\frac{a}{c}$; otherwise no solution.\n\nFor $c=0$, $d\\ne0$, $a\\ne0$: a single ray for every $k$. For $c=0$, $d\\ne0$, $a=0$: all real numbers when $\\frac{b}{d}<k$, otherwise no solution. For $c=d=0$: the expression is undefined everywhere.\n\nMultiply by $(cx+d)^2$ and classify the resulting polynomial; an empty solution set is neither of the requested nonempty forms.",
      "worked": "On the domain $cx+d\\ne0$, multiply by $(cx+d)^2>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}(ax+b)(cx+d)&<k(cx+d)^2\\\\(ax+b)(cx+d)-k(cx+d)^2&<0\\\\\\bigl((a-kc)x+b-kd\\bigr)(cx+d)&<0\\end{align*}$$\n\nFirst suppose $c\\ne0$ and put $D=ad-bc$, $p=-\\frac{d}{c}$.\n\nIf $D\\ne0$ and $k\\ne \\frac{a}{c}$, the other root is $r=\\frac{kd-b}{a-kc}$, distinct from $p$. The leading coefficient is $c(a-kc)=c^2(\\frac{a}{c}-k)$.\n\nFor $k<\\frac{a}{c}$, this coefficient is positive, giving the single interval $\\min(p,r)<x<\\max(p,r)$.\n\nFor $k>\\frac{a}{c}$, it is negative, giving $x<\\min(p,r)$ or $x>\\max(p,r)$, two disjoint intervals.\n\nFor $k=\\frac{a}{c}$ and $D\\ne0$, the polynomial inequality reduces to $D(x-p)>0$. Thus $x>p$ when $D>0$, or $x<p$ when $D<0$, a single interval.\n\nIf $c\\ne0$ and $D=0$, then $ax+b=(\\frac{a}{c})(cx+d)$. The original fraction equals $\\frac{a}{c}$ throughout its domain. For $k>\\frac{a}{c}$, every $x\\ne p$ is a solution, giving two intervals; for $k\\leq \\frac{a}{c}$, there is no solution.\n\nIf $c=0$ and $d\\ne0$, the inequality is $(\\frac{a}{d})x+\\frac{b}{d}<k$. For $a\\ne0$, put $h=\\frac{kd-b}{a}$: the solution is $x<h$ when $\\frac{a}{d}>0$, or $x>h$ when $\\frac{a}{d}<0$. Each is one interval.\n\nIf also $a=0$, the solution is $\\mathbb R$ when $\\frac{b}{d}<k$, and empty otherwise.\n\nIf $c=d=0$, the original denominator vanishes everywhere, so there is no admissible $x$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
